// Build-A-Player College Football, All-Time: defensive backs (the DB half of the defensive All-Time pool;
// data/cfb-dl-legends.js has DL, data/cfb-legends.js and data/cfb-te-legends.js the offense). Loaded like them,
// only when a build is All-Time. Written by the DL/DB legends build, 2026-11-25 (generator and notes kept with the build:
// scratchpad cfbdef/legends/tools: db_rows.py, the hand rows; build.py, Size, OVR, order and the tier step).
//
// ── Who is here ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 81 legends: 44 cornerbacks, 37 safeties, from 39 schools, each in his school's 2026 conference
// (data/cfb-teams.js codes; Colorado and Utah in the Big 12, Air Force in the Mountain West, Colorado State in the Pac-12):
//   SEC 17 CB + 10 S · Big Ten 9 CB + 10 S · Big 12 10 CB + 5 S · ACC 7 CB + 8 S · Pac-12 1 S · Mountain West 1 S · Independents 1 CB + 2 S
// Not every school has one: the list is the position's college greats. Who qualifies: every Thorpe Award winner
//   (41 men, Thomas Everett 1986 to Caleb Downs 2025, both 1987 co-winners), the Nagurski and Bednarik winners
//   who played the secondary, Charles Woodson's 1997 Heisman and Travis Hunter's 2024 one, the unanimous and consensus
//   All-Americans, the interception record holders (Gerod Holliman's 14 in 2014, Terrell Buckley's 501 return
//   yards, Terry Hoage's 12 in 1982), and the icons (Ronnie Lott, Kenny Easley, Ed Reed, Troy Polamalu, Sean Taylor).
//   Mostly 1980 to now; LSU has five (DBU), no other school more than four.
// Corner or safety: his college position. A nickel or star is a corner (Tyrann Mathieu); a safety-corner hybrid is
//   where he played his peak season (Minkah Fitzpatrick safety, Jalen Ramsey corner, Jabrill Peppers safety).
//   Travis Hunter is here for his corner play; his receiving is not graded. Chris Hudson played every spot in
//   Colorado's secondary; he is a safety here, where he played in the NFL. height / weight: as data/cfb-dl-legends.js
//   orders the sources (school listing, combine or pro day, else Wikipedia's listed weight: Hoage, Dixon, Figures,
//   Hudson, Lott, Kinard, Poindexter, Fletcher, Lawrence Wright, Jerry Gray, Carrier, Easley, Cecil).
//
// ── The row (data/cfb-players.js's shape for a defender, minus the season lines) ────────────────────────────
// starter: true on every row; depth: his order at his slot type at the school, the greatest (highest ovr) first;
//   slot: CB1 for a school's first corner, CB2 for the rest, S1 for a safety; pos 'db'; espnPos: CB or S.
// years, school, number, espn, line: as data/cfb-dl-legends.js describes them.
//
// ── Grades (0-11: F D C- C C+ B- B B+ A- A A+ S, the college scale of data/cfb-players.js and data/cfb-legends.js) ──
// Graded on what he did IN COLLEGE at his peak college season (the line: the honors and numbers used), never on his
// NFL career, and never copied from a rating anywhere: every trait was built from the evidence, 2026-11-25.
// Evidence, per man: his college numbers (ESPN's college stat pages for the men who played from about 2004 on, the
// schools' record books, NCAA leader lists, the schools' and the College Football Hall of Fame's bios), his awards
// and All-America teams (consensus and unanimous as the NCAA counts them), and his draft-class scouting (combine 40,
// 10-yard split, bench, height and weight; a college Speed follows his 40). Facts were checked against those sources
// and Wikipedia's facts; nothing was taken from a game's or a site's ratings.
// Trait by trait: Ball Skills from interceptions and passes defended per game (for his era) and return yards;
//   Man Coverage and Press from the receivers he was asked to take away, the touchdowns he allowed where charted, his
//   length and his honors; Fluidity and Speed from the 40 and the scouting; Zone IQ and Play Recognition from
//   interceptions in zone, tackles and the staff's word (a Thorpe winner's coaches); Run Support from his tackles,
//   TFL and forced fumbles (a box safety gets it: Lott, Roy Williams, Bob Sanders, Polamalu).
// S (11) only where it was generational: Woodson's ball skills, Deion Sanders' speed, fluidity and man coverage,
//   Ed Reed's ball skills and play recognition, Ronnie Lott's and Roy Williams' run support.
// Size is never graded by hand: data/dbs.js's dbSizeGrade on his height and weight against his spot (corner 175-210 lb
//   and 5'9"-6'3", 70% weight; safety 185-218 lb, 80% weight).
// ovr: utils/simulation.js calcOVRDB put through utils/cfbOvr.js collegeOVR, the game's own numbers.
// One tier step after the hand grades: the men at the top of the position (the consensus all-time names and the
//   multiple-award winners, about 20, build.py KEEP) keep their grades as written; every other row loses one grade on each trait of
//   A- or better outside his two signature traits, so a one-award All-American reads 85-89 and the greats pull away.
//   Set by hand after it: Deion Sanders, Ronnie Lott, Roy Williams, Terry Kinard and Terry Hoage up to where the
//   all-time lists have them; Caleb Downs, Kyle Hamilton and Patrick Surtain II down a step (short or recent peaks);
//   Cooper DeJean, Eric Weddle and Jalen Ramsey down a step; Bob Sanders' run support held at A+, not S; Greg Myers'
//   ball skills, zone and recognition up a step (two All-America years, 15 career interceptions).
// Speed step (last): where Wikipedia's pre-draft table has his 40, Speed is set from it (build.py SPEED40): 4.27 S
//   (Deion only), 4.28-4.37 A+, 4.40-4.43 A, 4.48-4.54 A-, 4.56-4.67 B+. Bob Sanders (4.40), Winfield (4.41), Huff (4.35)
//   and Polamalu (4.33) went up; Ed Reed (4.57), Sean Taylor (4.51), Bly, Dawkins and Walton down.
// Result, OVR p10 / median / p90 / max: cb 86/88/95/99 · s 85/88/97/97
//   (99: Charles Woodson, Deion Sanders).
// Rank order cross-checked against the names the all-time college lists agree on (ESPN's 150 greatest, the AP's
//   all-time All-America team, the College Football Hall of Fame; from those lists as known, not a fresh download):
//   Woodson, Sanders, Reed, Lott, Peterson, Berry, Easley and Bailey are the top end here.
//
// ── Headshots (ESPN only) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 15 rows have one: headshot "cfb_{id}" = his ESPN college-football photo (public/headshots/cfb_{id}.webp, fetched
//   the way scripts/fetch-cfb-assets.mjs does it). Only men whose ESPN college record has a photo (about 2015 on);
//   every one was looked at on a contact sheet. No NFL, Wikipedia or Hall of Fame photos: everyone else shows initials.
//   Tre'Vius Hodges-Tomlinson is "Tre Tomlinson" on ESPN now; the row keeps the name he played under.
//
// ── Checks (2026-11-25) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// On the output: every team is a data/cfb-teams.js code, no name twice, every trait 0-11, Size equals dbSizeGrade,
//   ovr equals the formula, and `npx esbuild` parses the file.
// Facts: ESPN's college stat pages confirmed the lines of the men from about 2015 on (Stingley's 6 INT, Delpit's 5 INT
//   and 5 sacks, Barron's 5 INT, DeJean's 3 pick-sixes, Watts' 7 INT, Hunter's 4 INT and 11 PD, Cason's 2007).
//   Every Thorpe year (1986-2025), Nagurski year and Bednarik year in a line was checked against Wikipedia's winner
//   tables: all match. Searched and confirmed in published sources: Buckley (12 INT in 1991, the NCAA's 501 career
//   return yards), Kinard (unanimous 1982, Clemson's 17 career INT), Rod Woodson (consensus 1986, Purdue's career
//   co-leader with 11), Greg Myers (consensus 1995, first-team All-American 1994, four-time All-WAC, 15 INT) and Chris
//   Hudson (consensus 1994, "could play any position in the backfield"). Every ESPN id was checked against ESPN's athlete
//   record (name, college, position). Sports-reference's pages could not be read from the build (a bot check).
//   Not confirmed: jersey numbers, and the college weights of the men listed above under the row (Wikipedia's listed
//   weights, which can be a pro weight).

